# Ruby on Rails security

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic security rules to
Rails-specific, OWASP-relevant risks and the safe framework API to use
instead. Applies only to `*.rb` files.

## Mass assignment and strong parameters

- Never build or update a model from a raw `params` hash
  (`Model.new(params[:model])`, `record.update(params)`); require the
  permitted shape first with `params.expect(model: [:field, ...])`
  (current Rails idiom) or `params.require(:model).permit(:field, ...)`,
  and never call `permit!` on user-controlled params — it disables
  strong parameters entirely and lets any attacker-supplied key through,
  including ones like `admin`/`role` that were never meant to be
  user-settable.
- List every permitted attribute explicitly; do not permit a whole
  nested hash with a bare `permit(:nested)` when only specific
  sub-keys should be writable.

## SQL and data access

- Use ActiveRecord's parameterized conditions (`where("age > ?", age)`,
  `where(age: age)`, or named placeholders) — never
  string-interpolate a user-influenced value into `where`,
  `find_by_sql`, `order`, or any other query method
  (`where("name = '#{params[:name]}'")` is SQL injection).
- When raw SQL genuinely cannot be avoided (a fragment passed to
  `order`/`pluck` built from a dynamic column list, for instance), pass
  untrusted values through `ActiveRecord::Base.sanitize_sql`/
  `sanitize_sql_array`, never through direct string interpolation.

## Output encoding and XSS

- Rely on ERB's automatic HTML escaping for any value that can contain
  user input; using `raw(value)` or `value.html_safe` on
  user-controlled or user-influenced content reintroduces XSS by
  marking untrusted text as pre-escaped. Reserve `raw`/`html_safe` for
  genuinely static, developer-authored content, and prefer a sanitizer
  (`sanitize`) over `html_safe` when some user-supplied markup must be
  allowed through.

## Authentication and authorization

- Every controller action that requires a logged-in user runs a
  `before_action` (e.g. `:authenticate_user!` or the app's own
  equivalent) — an action added to an already-protected controller
  inherits the filter only if it is not explicitly skipped
  (`skip_before_action`); check that a new action was not accidentally
  excluded.
- Authentication (who is this) is not authorization (what can they do):
  confirm the current user is allowed to act on *this specific* record
  (ownership/role check), not just that they are logged in at all —
  an unauthorized action on someone else's resource (IDOR) is a
  distinct bug from a missing login check.

## CSRF

- Keep `protect_from_forgery with: :exception` (or the equivalent
  `ActionController::Base` default) enabled for any controller serving
  browser-submitted forms; do not disable CSRF protection
  (`skip_before_action :verify_authenticity_token`,
  `protect_from_forgery with: :null_session`) on an action reachable
  from a standard HTML form just to make a request easier to test or
  call from a script — scope any real exception (a webhook endpoint
  verified by its own signature, for instance) as narrowly as possible
  and say why in the change.

## Background jobs

- An `ActiveJob` must tolerate being run more than once for the same
  logical unit of work (most production queue adapters are at-least-once) —
  guard non-idempotent side effects (charging a card, sending an email)
  with a uniqueness check or an idempotency key rather than assuming
  single delivery.

## Secrets

- Never commit a credential, API key, or signing secret in source —
  store it in Rails encrypted credentials (`bin/rails credentials:edit`)
  or an environment variable the deployment already manages, and load it
  through `Rails.application.credentials`/`ENV`, not a literal in a
  `.rb` file.
